2. Notice

The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all
copies or substantial portions of the Software.

3. Attribution

This condition is a condition of the grant in section 1. Any permission granted
above is conditioned on the following being satisfied by anyone who uses,
copies, modifies, merges, publishes, distributes, sublicenses, sells, or builds
hardware from the Software or a derivative work:

   (a) They must credit the tooling that produced this work, in the form

           Built with DeepSeek V4.1 Flash running DeepSeek Harness (DSH)

       or the shorter, equivalent form

           Made with DeepSeek V4.1 Flash on DSH

   (b) The credit must be reasonably visible to the recipient of the Software or
       of the hardware built from it. Any ONE of the following is sufficient:

         - the README or the top-level documentation of the project that uses it;
         - a NOTICE, CREDITS or attribution file shipped with it;
         - an "about", "credits" or "acknowledgements" screen, page or section, if
           the project has one;
         - for a hardware design: the top-level RTL module header comments, the
           register-map or datasheet document, or the package/die documentation  - 
           wherever the design credits its other contributors.

       Silicon is stricter than this: a Silicon Product must also satisfy
       section 4, and where the two overlap section 4 wins.

   (c) The credit must name both things: the model and the harness. "DeepSeek",
       "an AI", or "automatically generated" alone does not satisfy this
       condition. Keeping the hyperlinks to
       https://huggingface.co/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017 and
       https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness next to the credit
       satisfies it as well.

   (d) The credit must not be used to suggest that the copyright holders or
       DeepSeek endorse, sponsor, or take responsibility for the project that
       uses it.

4. Silicon attribution: the marks go on the chip

This condition is a condition of the grant in section 1, in addition to
section 3. It applies to every Silicon Product: any integrated circuit, die,
chiplet, multi-die assembly, packaged part or module, and any FPGA or other
programmable-device configuration image, that is made from, implements, or
contains the Software, a derivative work, or any part of `beyond/` (its RTL, its
testbenches, and the designs they describe). Anyone who makes, has made, sells,
transfers or otherwise distributes a Silicon Product (the "maker") must:

   (a) Put the DeepSeek logo and the line from (b) on the Silicon Product
       itself:
         - on every die the maker has fabricated (for a multi-die assembly: on
           each of those dies), as in-die marks in a visible layer; and
         - on the package or module. Where the maker does not make the package
           itself  -  an FPGA-based board, a chiplet in a third party's package  - 
           the equivalent mark goes on the board, module or enclosure the maker
           does ship.
       The logo is the official DeepSeek lockup included with this repository at
       `docs/img/deepseek-logo.svg`: the whale mark and the wordmark together.
       Its shape, proportions and the relative position of the two must be
       unchanged. Monochrome is fine and the ink may follow the surface (black on
       light, white on dark); in a single-layer in-die mark, the whale's eye is a
       cutout, which is not a change of shape. The logo must not be stretched,
       squashed, rotated, mirrored, cropped, redrawn, or split so that the whale
       appears without the wordmark.

   (b) Put this line on the package marking, in full and as type:

           Built with DeepSeek V4.1 Flash running DeepSeek Harness (DSH)

       It must be readable as text: a QR code, barcode, URL, serial number or a
       document the part merely points to does not satisfy this paragraph on its
       own.

   (c) Put the same logo and the same line on the datasheet, on its first page
       or cover, and on the product page if the maker publishes one.

   Size and legibility. The test is that a person with normal corrected vision
   can read the whole line without a magnifier and without squinting. The line
   may be wrapped over several lines; the cap height below applies to each line.

         - package or module: the line at a cap height of at least 1.2 mm and
           the logo at least 3.0 mm wide, on a face that is normally visible
           when the part is installed. On a package whose marked face is
           smaller than 8 mm across  -  where those numbers cannot physically
           fit  -  the floor is a 0.9 mm cap height and a 2.0 mm logo, still as
           type. If the part ships with a permanently attached heatsink, lid,
           shield or enclosure, the same marks must appear on its outer surface
           as well;
         - die: the logo and the line present and legible under standard optical
           inspection at 100x. Naked-eye legibility is carried by the package and
           the datasheet, because an in-die mark is microscopic by nature;
         - documentation: the line at a cap height of at least 4 mm (about
           12 pt), in a colour that contrasts with the background, on the first
           page or cover  -  not only in a footnote or end-credits page, and not
           smaller than the body text used for the document's other credits.

   (d) A Silicon Product distributed without the marks required by (a), (b) or
       (c) is not licensed under this license. Note also that GDSII, OASIS, mask
       sets, netlists, bitstreams, HDL and the RTL in `beyond/` are "Software"
       for the purposes of section 3, so the credit there applies to them as
       well.

   (e) The DeepSeek name and logo are the property of their owner. This
       condition requires their use solely to identify the provenance of the
       design and grants no trademark rights beyond that use; it does not imply
       endorsement (see 3(d)).

5. Not required

Sections 3 and 4 do not apply to: private use that is not distributed or made
available to anyone outside the organisation using it; quoting a portion of this
Software in a discussion, review, benchmark comparison or academic work, where
citing the project is already normal practice; or private, undistributed
engineering samples, shuttle runs and internal qualification parts that are never
sold, given away or otherwise transferred outside the organisation.

6. Conditions from other licenses still apply in full

Portions of this repository are licensed differently and their terms are
unaffected by sections 3 and 4  -  in particular `src/hashx/hashx.c`
(**LGPL-3.0**, from tevador/hashx) and `third_party/argon2` (CC0-1.0 /
Apache-2.0). See THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md. Where a third-party license conflicts
with section 3 or 4, that licence wins for the third-party portion.

